    "turn_text": "My name is Elhan Omar. I am a state representative for Minneapolis's District 60B, and I serve as the Assistant Minority Leader. Conversations About Home at the Deportation Center by Wassan Shere. Well, I think home spat me out. The black outs and curfews like the tongue against loose tooth. God, do you know how difficult it is? To talk about the day your own city dragged you by the hair, past the old prison, past the school gates, past the burning torsals erected on poles like flags. When I meet others like me, I recognize the longing, the missing, the memory of ash on their faces. No one leaves home unless home is the mouth of a shark. I've been carrying the old anthem in my mouth for so long that there is no space for another song, another tongue, or another language. I know a shame that shrouds totally engulfs. I tore up and ate my own passport in an airport hotel. I'm bloated with language I can't afford to forget. Oh. A lot of it resonates with me, you know, Worsen and I both come from Somalia and I think in this story that is one that either ourselves or the people we know have gone through. I went through the process of fleeing a war at the age of eight. and a lot of my memories of leaving home have a lot to do with like seeing war and death and remembering the fears in people's faces. and struggling to comprehend how a perfectly beautiful neighborhood and neighbors turn into monsters and a place of pain and destruction and chaos. And I think in this poem that comes to life, God, do you know how difficult it is to talk about the day your own city, dread you by the hair, past the old prison, past the school gates, past the burning torsals erected on poles like flags. When I meet others like me, I recognize the longing, the missing, the memory of ash on their faces. When I think about home, I think about my grandfather. I think about the smell of coffee. I think about the sounds of intriguing conversations. I think about, I think about forgiveness. our home when I was younger with right next to the local market and our house actually laid right next to the meat shop. And everyone with their own baggage in history stopped by our front porch to have bun, which is coffee, with my grandfather. And it was a place where people were forgiven for their history and for their past and were allowed to be seen as people, as persons who were worthy of sharing their stories without judgment. And for me, home has always been and is a place where you are fully accepted and forgiven for whatever that is wrong or inadequate or disappointing about you. Home is a place where you're forgiven so that you start anew. Conversations about home at the Deportation Center by Wassen Shere. Well, I think home spat me out. The black outs and curfews like tongue against loose tooth. God, do you know how difficult it is to talk about the day your own city dragged you by the hair, past the old prison, past the old gates, past the burning torsoles erected on poles like flags? When I meet others like me, I recognize the longing, the missing, the memory of ash on their faces. No one leaves home unless home is the mouth of a shark. I've been carrying the old anthem in my mouth for so long that there is no space for another song, another tongue, or another language. I know a shame that shrouds totally engulfs. I tore up and ate my own passport in an airport hotel. I'm bloated with language. I can't afford to forget.",
